ild_calls": 0, "edit_calls": 2, "ended_on_rejection": false, "error": null, "exec_mode": "local", "failure_class": "solved", "failure_reason": null, "faults_served": 0, "final_answer_complete": true, "final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "json", "fresh_subagents": 0, "harness": "omp", "harness_version": "11.9.0 (/workspace/pi-check/cache2/bin/omp/ab1db22d1d3f60e8f443006a5aa3d5b4158aa3cf/omp)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "The shell is mangling indentation; I'll use a script file.\nI fixed the bug in `ledger/currency.py`. All 4 tests in `tests/test_totals.py` pass now, and so does `tests/test_report.py`. pytest isn't installed here, so I ran the test functions directly with a small script.\n\n**Cause:** `to_cents` used `int(amount * 100)`, which cuts off the decimals. Many prices can't be stored exactly as floats, so the result lands just under the right number and gets cut down by a cent: `19.99 * 100` is `1998.9999999999998`, which became `1998`, and `0.29 * 100` is `28.999999999999996`, which became `28`. Everything downstream (subtotal, discount, tax) then started from the wrong number of cents.\n\n**Fix:**\n```python\nreturn int(round(amount * 100))\n```\n\n**One limit:** if a price has more than two decimal places and sits exactly halfway between two cents (e.g. `0.125`), Python's `round()` goes to the even cent, so it becomes 12 cents, not 13. Some halfway values are also stored slightly off as floats, so they can round either way. With normal two-decimal prices this never matters. If prices can have more decimals, `to_cents` should switch to `Decimal(str(amount)).quantize(Decimal(\"0.01\"), ROUND_HALF_UP)`, which matches the round-half-up rule already used in `discounts.py`. I didn't make that cha